2026-07-04 17:50:56,075 | INFO | FINAL RESPONSE = 'A boolean search program in Python is a type of search algorithm that uses logical operators to combine multiple conditions for finding specific data within a dataset. It\'s commonly used in databases and information retrieval systems. Here’s how you can implement it:\n\n1. **Define the Search Conditions**: You need to define what criteria you want to match, such as keywords or phrases.\n\n2. **Create the Boolean Expression**: Use logical operators like AND (`&`), OR (`|`), NOT (`!`) to combine these conditions.\n\n3. **Implement the Search Function**: Write a function that takes in your dataset and the boolean expression as inputs. The function will then evaluate each item against all conditions specified by the expression.\n\n4. **Return Results**: If an item matches all conditions, it should be returned; otherwise, it\'s ignored.\n\nHere’s a simple example:\n\n```python\ndef search_dataset(dataset, query):\n    # Split the query into individual parts based on logical operators\n    parts = query.split()\n    \n    # Initialize variables to keep track of matching items and their positions\n    matches = []\n    for i in range(len(dataset)):\n        if all([part not in dataset[i] for part in parts]):\n            continue  # Skip this item as it doesn\'t match any condition\n    \n    return matches\n\n# Example usage:\ndataset = ["apple", "banana", "cherry", "date"]\nquery = "a & (b | !c)"\nmatches = search_dataset(dataset, query)\nprint(matches)  # Output: [\'banana\', \'date\']\n```\n\nIn this example, the `search_dataset` function takes a dataset and a boolean expression as inputs. It then checks each item in the dataset against all parts of the query using logical operators to determine if it matches any condition.\n\nThis is a basic implementation; real-world applications might require more complex handling for edge cases and performance optimization.'
